// Chapter 8's review item bank: the questions the spaced-repetition queue draws on
// (docs/design/adaptive.md). Every item names exactly one objective from objectives.js, and the items
// an objective gets differ in what they demand — say it, explain it, or use it on a case the chapter
// has not worked through — so a reader who has memorised one still has to think about the others.
//
// The `why` on a wrong option is the working part of this file. It says what choosing that option
// reveals about the reader's thinking, so the agent round can turn "wrong three times" into a named
// misconception and teach against it. A distractor that is merely false teaches nothing.
//
// Six ideas in this chapter are the ones readers get wrong, and the distractors are built out of them
// rather than out of plausible-sounding falsehoods.
//
//   An experiment's force is in what it rules out. Avery's enzymes and Hershey and Chase's labels each
//     eliminated candidates, each left a different doubt open, and a label marks an element, not a
//     molecule's fate; Meselson and Stahl's first generation and second generation each ruled out a
//     different scheme.
//   Complementary is not identical, and pairing is geometry. The two strands are partners, not copies
//     or mirror images; a polymerase judges the shape of a pair, not the count of its hydrogen bonds;
//     and a template works only because each base has one partner.
//   A polymerase extends, never starts, and only at a 3′ end. Primers, the lagging strand, the energy
//     arriving on the incoming nucleotide, pyrophosphate splitting as the pull, proofreading and the end
//     of a linear chromosome all follow from it.
//   A rate needs a denominator. One in a billion per base pair, fewer than one per daughter cell and
//     seventy per child are the same process counted per different things, and the checks multiply
//     rather than stand in for one another.
//   Danger is not frequency, and a copying error is not damage. Lost purines are commonest, double-
//     strand breaks most dangerous; mismatch repair deals with copying errors and needs to know the new
//     strand, excision repair deals with damage and needs the partner strand.
//   Genome size is not gene count, and activity is not function. Most of a genome is transposable
//     elements and their remains, spreading because they copy themselves; "junk" is a claim about a
//     particular sequence, to be tested.
//
// Others in the same family, each of which a distractor below encodes: that information is a matter
// of size; that possibilities add where they multiply; that a few heat-killed bacteria survived; that
// the transferred trait is a coat of borrowed material; that an isotope has chemical preferences; that
// a density label is a radioactive one; that a diffraction pattern is a picture of the molecule; that
// 5′ and 3′ are counts; that antiparallel templates alone force fragments, whatever the polymerase;
// that ATP pays for each nucleotide; that topoisomerase joins Okazaki fragments; that forks from one
// origin number one or four; that one bacterium's copying time holds for every bacterium; that
// mismatch repair finds abnormal bases; that ultraviolet damage is the
// commonest; that thymine pairs more strongly than uracil; that histones read the bases; that
// heterochromatin is not copied; that mitochondria carry telomerase; that cells stop when their
// telomeres are used up; that a larger genome hides undiscovered genes; and that a transcribed stretch
// must be a gene.
//
// Formats:
//   mcq  — { options: [{ text, correct } | { text, why }] }, exactly one correct.
//   free — { rubric: [...] }, the points a good answer makes.
//
// No `task` items. Chapter 8 ships at reduced scope, cut to four figures, with no figure tasks, so
// every objective's items are questions: 120 in all, 80 mcq and 40 free, three per objective and both
// formats for each — the `-1` and `-2` items multiple choice and the `-3` item free. No item cites a
// figure by number, since the numbers moved when the chapter was cut and would move again, and no item
// needs a figure to answer it. The `apply` objectives' items use cases the chapter does not work
// through: other genomes, sizes and speeds, mutant strains, drugs, diseases and imagined organisms.
//
// Option order and length: the correct option is spread across A to D, and each item's options are
// written to comparable lengths, so that neither the position nor the length of an option says which
// one is right. A reader learns a position or a length faster than a mechanism. Measured on this
// revision by counting each option's characters with the markup stripped, a tie counted as neither
// longer nor shorter: the correct option is strictly the longest of its four in 20 of the 80 items,
// the second longest in 19, the third in 21 and strictly the shortest in 20, with no ties; it
// sits at A, B, C and D 20, 20, 20 and 20 times. Nor does the size give it away: where it is the
// longest it is at most 10 per cent longer than the longest wrong option, and where it is the
// shortest the shortest wrong option is at most 10 per cent longer than it. Whoever adds an item
// keeps all three. The written order is not the order a reader sees, because options are shown in a
// drawn order, so no explanation, option or `why` names an option by where it is written; each names
// it by what it says.
//
// Numbers come from the chapter. Where an item uses a case the chapter does not state, the case is
// either set as an imagined one and says so, or checked against a named source:
//   Avery, MacLeod & McCarty, J. Exp. Med. 79:137 (1944) — the enzyme against the type III capsular
//     polysaccharide left the extract's activity intact (`i-avery-enzymes-2`).
//   Sinsheimer, J. Mol. Biol. 1:43 (1959) — a small phage's single-stranded DNA, A 24.6, T 32.7,
//     G 24.1, C 18.5 per cent, breaking Chargaff's equalities (`i-chargaff-rules-2`).
//   Watson & Crick, Nature 171:964 (1953) — rare forms of the bases as a source of mispairing
//     (`i-pairing-geometry-2`).
//   Sanger, Nicklen & Coulson, PNAS 74:5463 (1977) — chain termination by dideoxy nucleotides
//     (`i-polymerase-requirements-2`); Elion et al., PNAS 74:5716 (1977) — acyclovir built into viral
//     DNA and activated by a viral enzyme (`i-polymerase-requirements-3`).
//   Kornberg & Baker, DNA Replication, 2nd ed. (1992) — temperature-sensitive primase and ligase
//     mutants, and quinolones acting on bacterial topoisomerases (`i-primer-needed-2`,
//     `i-lagging-strand-2`, `i-fork-machinery-2`).
//   Friedberg et al., DNA Repair and Mutagenesis, 2nd ed. (2006) — bulky adducts from tobacco smoke
//     and cooked meat removed by nucleotide excision repair, strains lacking the GATC methylase as
//     mutators, and inherited sensitivity to ionising radiation (`i-excision-repair-1`,
//     `i-mismatch-repair-2`, `i-repair-choice-1`, `-3`); Ionov et al., Nature 363:558 (1993) — repeats
//     changing length in tumours lacking mismatch repair (`i-repair-choice-3`).
//   Al-Tassan et al., Nat. Genet. 30:227 (2002) — MUTYH, base excision of adenine opposite oxidised
//     guanine, and inherited colon polyps (`i-repair-choice-2`).
//   Nowoshilow et al., Nature 554:50 (2018) — the axolotl's genome, about 32 billion pairs per set
//     (`i-packing-problem-1`); GRCh38 — chromosome 21, about 46.7 million pairs (`i-packing-problem-2`).
//   Balhorn, Genome Biol. 8:227 (2007) — protamines, arginine-rich, replacing histones in sperm
//     (`i-nucleosome-3`); Muller, J. Genet. 22:299 (1930) — a fly's eye-colour gene moved beside
//     heterochromatin and silenced in patches (`i-packing-levels-2`).
//   Lin et al., Mol. Microbiol. 10:923 (1993) — the linear chromosome of Streptomyces; Bao & Cohen,
//     Genes Dev. 15:1518 (2001) — the proteins bound at its ends that fill the last gap
//     (`i-end-replication-problem-3`).
//   Vulliamy et al., Nature 413:432 (2001) — dyskeratosis congenita and telomerase RNA; marrow, skin,
//     nails and mouth (`i-telomerase-2`).
//   Kazazian et al., Nature 332:164 (1988) — new LINE-1 insertions in the factor VIII gene of two
//     unrelated patients with haemophilia A (`i-transposable-elements-2`).
//   Ibarra-Laclette et al., Nature 498:94 (2013) — Utricularia gibba, about 82 million pairs and about
//     28,500 genes, a typical number for a plant (`i-c-value-paradox-1`); Nóbrega et al., Nature
//     431:988 (2004) — mice lacking two long gene-poor stretches, which held more than a thousand
//     sequences conserved between humans and rodents, with no evident defect (`i-junk-dna-debate-2`).
// Imagined cases, with their numbers stated in the item: the 6-million-pair bacterium and the
// 150-million-pair chromosome at 30 nucleotides a second (`i-origins-arithmetic-*`), the 5-billion-pair
// genome (`i-one-in-a-billion-1`), the 200-pair nucleosome repeat (`i-nucleosome-2`), the telomere
// lengths and losses of `i-divisions-counted-1` and `-3`, the 1.5-billion-pair plant
// (`i-genome-composition-2`), and the enzymes and organisms that do not exist: the polymerase that
// adds at either end, the 3′-to-5′ polymerase, the uracil-DNA organism and the two-partner bases.
//
// Written against the chapter on branch ch08-dna at 5126afa, then read against the prose fix on branch
// ch08 at cb447d7. That fix changed no objective's id or statement and no number an item uses, except
// that §8.5 now says "a small fraction" of the electrons on the respiratory chain leak, not "a per cent
// or two", and `i-dna-damage-2` follows it. Then corrected by the accuracy review against OpenStax
// Biology 2e of 2026-09-24, findings 14 to 39 and the item writer's points it adjudicated. The same
// review changed two objectives: `dna-damage` asks for a rate only for the kinds of damage that do not
// depend on sunlight, and `packing-levels` asks which chromatin holds most of the genes being read and
// what happens to nucleosomes when the DNA is copied. It also corrected the prose on parallel strands:
// they would not remove the lagging strand but move it, to the fork leaving the origin the other way,
// and only a polymerase able to add at either end removes it at every fork; `i-lagging-strand-1` asks
// about the polymerase and `-3` about both. Two passages are avoided rather than relied on: `i-dna-damage-*` give no rate for
// ultraviolet dimers, which depends on the light, and none for single-strand breaks, which the chapter
// does not state; and the telomere items count from the shortest telomere, which reconciles ten
// thousand pairs at birth, fifty lost a division and Hayflick's fifty (`i-divisions-counted-3` asks
// exactly that).
//
// Typography follows the chapter: primes are U+2032 (5′, 3′); a number and its unit, per cent
// included, are joined by a no-break space, written \u00A0 (0.34\u00A0nm, 6\u00A0µm,
// 30\u00A0per\u00A0cent), as are the parts of a number in scientific notation
// (2\u00A0×\u00A010<sup>10</sup>), a numeral and its million or billion (6.2\u00A0billion), a
// section or chromosome and its number (Section\u00A08.3, chromosome\u00A01), a primed carbon
// and the word after it (3′\u00A0end, 5′\u00A0to\u00A03′), E.\u00A0coli and Franklin's
// A\u00A0form; a word joiner, \u2060, keeps LINE-\u20601 on one line; and species names are
// italic.
